This is an owner decision on accepted economics (K-01), so the smallest change depends on what is kept. In order of least change to the accepted design:

1. Stop the single-wallet loop in src/KilimAuction.sol _bid, right after _handleBid (line 131), by rewarding only what the caller adds over their own previous top bid:

    (address outbid, uint256 outbidAmount) = _handleBid(amount);
    uint256 rewarded = outbid == msg.sender ? amount - outbidAmount : amount;
    uint256 reward = rewardForBid(rewarded);

(The site quote must then call rewardForBid with the same increment, or expose a quote view that takes the bidder.) Two colluding wallets can still alternate, so this alone does not protect the pool.

2. Add a real per-bid cap so one bid or one short ladder cannot take a large share: set LAUNCH_MAX_SYMBOLS_PER_BID in script/DeployKilim.s.sol:80 and maxSymbolsPerBid in deploy/mainnet.example.json:26 well below symbolCap (for example 5,000, the reward of a 5 ETH bid). With that value, exhausting 1,000,000 symbols needs at least 200 bids at or above 5 ETH on a 10% ladder, which pushes the top bid far beyond any plausible price instead of 93.862 ETH.

3. If the owner wants K-01 unchanged, size symbolCap so it cannot bind at a plausible price, or state on the site that the pool can run out and that one bidder can take all of it with refunded ETH.

In every case: correct AUDIT.md:39 (K-01) from "cannot exhaust a pool" to the ETH numbers (pool = 1,000 ETH of cumulative bids; one actor exhausts it in one transaction at a 93.862 ETH top bid, about 7.3M gas), record the decision in launch/DECISIONS.md:26, and keep the site passing the quoted reward as minimumReward so no supporter is silently given fewer symbols than shown.

